From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scrrun: FileSystemObject's File, Folder and Drive objects and collections work

Scripting.FileSystemObject is how most scripts touch files, and its
object model was half there: a Folder's Size, Type, dates, attributes,
parent, drive, short names, Copy, Move, Delete and renaming were
E_NOTIMPL, as were a File's renaming, short names, drive, parent, type,
last access date, Copy, Move and Delete; SubFolders.Add and finding a
subfolder, file or drive by name in its collection; a Drive's Path,
ShareName, RootFolder and setting its volume name; GetDrive of a
\\server\share; MoveFolder. MoveFile took no wildcards.

- Folder: Size (everything in it, all the way down), Type and File.Type
  as Explorer names them, DateCreated/LastModified/LastAccessed (local
  time), Attributes (read; the read-only, hidden, system and archive ones
  set), IsRootFolder, ParentFolder (Nothing at a root), Drive, ShortPath,
  ShortName, Name (renames in place), Copy, Move (into a folder when the
  destination ends with a separator), Delete (force: read-only too).
- File: Name, ShortPath, ShortName, Drive, ParentFolder,
  DateLastAccessed, Type, Copy, Move, Delete; a moved or renamed object's
  Path follows it.
- Collections: SubFolders.Add; SubFolders.Item, Files.Item and
  Drives.Item by name (or letter).
- Drive: Path ("C:" or "\\server\share"), DriveLetter (none for a share),
  ShareName (a mapped drive's share), RootFolder, VolumeName set;
  GetDrive("\\server\share").
- MoveFile and MoveFolder take wildcards, and a destination ending with a
  separator is the folder to move into, as on Windows.

Wine's scrrun filesystem tests: no failures, as before.

Gate: test/fsoitems-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit cscript with
test/fsoitems.vbs). Mutant SG_MUTANT_NO_MOVEFOLDER (scrrun/filesystem.c).
